  "body": "The conjecture misreads the rule; the rule as written and the code do not part company. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The conjecture treats \"when any fence holds one\" as \"when any fence holds at least one object\", and then expects the last object inside a two-object fence to be scored. But \"holds one\" is singular: a fenced span that contains two JSON objects does not parse as one object, so no fence holds one, and the code correctly takes the else branch and returns the last top-level object, {\"z\": 3}. The code's fenced pool is the raw fenced span, not the individual objects inside it; it only uses a fenced candidate when that span parses as a single object. Therefore the machine's return of {\"z\": 3} for Reply A is the rule as written, not a divergence.\n\nA next conjecture should test the actual fenced branch: a reply with one fenced JSON object followed by a later top-level object, expecting the fenced object to win; or two separate fenced objects, expecting the last fenced object. It should avoid repeating the claim that a fence containing multiple objects triggers the fenced branch or that the code splits a fenced span into individual objects.\n\nReading 3a2e70008af12b34 is defective. It claims to render the conjecture's expectation, but it sets \"expect\": \"moves\". The conjecture's prose says: \"The check, however, returns {\"z\": 3} for both replies\" and \"I would be wrong if the check returns {\"y\": 2} for Reply A, or if it returns different answers for the two replies, or if it returns anything other than {\"z\": 3} for either reply.\" That is an expectation of unchanged output, not a move. The reading also gives only Reply A as input, omitting Reply B, even though the prose's claim is about both replies. The defect is in the reading, not the conjecture: the prose's own words contradict the reading's \"moves\" expectation.